=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch4

diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the VLAN 5 trunk sub-interface (`eth0.5`) on **switch4**, which is the access-layer switch serving PC1, PC2, and dc_pc5 (Campus 2 VLAN 5). This interface is in state **DOWN / STP disabled**, while its VLAN 6 counterpart (`eth0.6`) is fully UP and forwarding.

**Mechanism:** switch4 implements inter-VLAN segmentation using separate Linux bridges per VLAN: `br_vlan5` (VLAN 5, access ports eth1/eth2/eth3/eth6) and `br_vlan6` (VLAN 6, access ports eth4/eth5). The trunk toward sshswitch runs on eth0, with VLAN-tagged sub-interfaces `eth0.5` and `eth0.6` bridged into the respective per-VLAN bridges. When a VLAN 5 PC sends a frame, it enters `br_vlan5` normally but the only egress path toward the gateway — `eth0.5` — is DOWN, so every frame is silently dropped inside the bridge. VLAN 6 traffic flows unimpeded because `eth0.6` is up and forwarding.

**Localization:** Fault is entirely on **switch4**, interface `eth0.5` (the VLAN-tagged sub-interface on the `ms1_sw4` trunk link). sshswitch is healthy: it already has `bond0.5` up, `br_v5` constructed, and the gateway IP `10.5.0.254/24` assigned. PC1's ping to 10.5.0.254 shows 100% loss; PC3's ping to 10.6.0.254 succeeds 100%.

**Root cause:** The VLAN 5 tagged trunk sub-interface `eth0.5` on switch4 was not brought up (left in admin-down state), severing VLAN 5 egress from the switch while VLAN 6 was correctly initialised.

**Corrective fix:** On switch4, run:
```
ip link set eth0.5 up
```
This will immediately restore VLAN 5 forwarding from switch4 to sshswitch, allowing PC1/PC2/dc_pc5 to reach their gateway 10.5.0.254 and beyond.
=== END ===